From b74d5bb433f609e1206ec0e2e9dc97ea9de17b73 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: olympia <olympia@xbmc.org>
Date: Tue, 27 Oct 2026 22:12:01 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] [Video] Skip the local information question if the answer has
 no effect

Refreshing an item from the information dialog asked whether to ignore
the locally stored information and refresh from the internet, also when
there was nothing local at all. The check for a usable NFO became
"anything but an NFO error" in 28494c07d3, which let the no NFO case
through as well.

For movies and music videos the question now comes when the NFO or the
embedded tags can be used or when there is item or folder art next to
the file, the set art next to a movie included, as the scanner reads
those only with local information. The check follows the scanner,
within the artwork level setting, and lists the folder of the file
before the question, which the scan does again when it uses local
information. Actor thumbs in .actors and the art in the movie set
information folder are used whatever the answer is, so they do not
count.

TV shows, seasons and episodes are still asked without an NFO, because
there the answer can also cover the episode NFOs and the season art.
The same goes for an item listed as a folder in the file view, and for
a movie or music video in the folder that has its own content setting,
scanned recursively with folder names, where the NFO lookup of the job
differs from the scan and can miss the NFO the scan uses.

Backported to Kodi 21.3-Omega (a3a448d) for the OSMC vero5 build from
xbmc/xbmc#30169 (e27613b). Omega names are used: CONTENT_* for the
content types, CInfoScanner::NO_NFO and ERROR_NFO, MediaTypeMovie,
MediaTypeMusicVideo and MediaTypeVideoCollection. Omega has no hybrid
folders, so m_bIsFolder of the item stands for
VIDEO::IsBrowsableFolder(). The condition in Omega has no exception for
the metadata.local scraper, which comes from another change, and none is
added.

Omega looks for the local art inside CVideoInfoScanner::GetArtwork():
with folder names it also checks the folder of the file, then the local
art base filename, both with CVideoInfoScanner::AddLocalItemArtwork(),
which is made public for this. HasLocalArt() follows that. Unlike the
second paragraph says, in Omega the scanner reads the art in the movie
set information folder only with local information too, so for a movie
that is not a plugin item it counts as well, looked up for the set the
movie is in now.
